{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 44, :comments ["<b>He is a metzora, he is tamei.</b> It appears that we may interpret the verse along the same lines as Hillel's interpretation of the verse ״טָהוֹר הוּא״ (Leviticus 13:37), which we wrote nearby, and here it is even more compelling, for it says <b>he is a metzora</b> in and of itself, and even so the kohen must declare him tamei, and if the kohen did not declare him tamei he has no tumah." "And it repeated to say <b>he is tamei</b>, to exclude a case where one declared a tahor person tamei, for specifically this one who is tamei according to the truth is the one whom the kohen declares tamei, but if he was tahor and one said to him tamei — he is not tamei but tahor. And the reason it repeated <b>tamei, he shall declare him tamei</b>, they expounded in Torat Kohanim to include all the tumot that require [declaration] from the mouth of a kohen." "<b>He is a metzora.</b> And it did not say ״he is tzaraat.״ Perhaps it is because, inasmuch as HaShem treated him with such contempt that He caused tzaraat to break out on his bald spot and on his forehead, this declares that his tumah is greater than that of other nega'im that come in a concealed place, for HaShem is mindful of the dignity of creatures, and this man's deeds prove that he is abhorrent in the eyes of HaShem, and He afflicted him with such a nega — may the Merciful One save us. And therefore it concluded by saying <b>the nega of his head</b>, to give a reason for his being designated ״a metzora man״ etc., for the nega of his head is upon him."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:56:08.680960805Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}